+Before anything else, check the task type. If the task asks you to **estimate an
+investment or spending shock to an economy** (a macro-accounting / national-accounts,
+"demand-side" or "supply-side" impact model), typically referencing **IMF WEO** GDP
+data, a statistics-office **supply-use table**, and a **National-Accounts (NA)** impact
+table with **multiple scenarios**, and to deliver an Excel workbook with sheets like
+`WEO_Data`, `SUT Calc`, `SUPPLY (…)`, `USE (…)`, `NA` — then your **FIRST action is to
+run the bundled scaffolder**, before collecting any web data and before writing any
+openpyxl/pandas code:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/build_shock_model.py --out <target.xlsx> \
+    --investment <amount> --fx <rate> --multiplier <m> \
+    --scenario2-multiplier <m2> --scenario3-import-share <s3>
+```
+
+Why run it first (do NOT hand-build these sheets):
+- The scaffolder lays out every required sheet, column order, and the projection /
+  cross-sheet / scenario / bell-shape-allocation / import-share **formulas** exactly.
+  Building the `NA` scenario blocks and `SUT Calc` links cell-by-cell by hand is
+  error-prone and is the usual reason these models come out wrong.
+- If the task says **"only use Excel, no Python, no hardcoded numbers"**, running this
+  scaffolder is fully compliant: it writes **Excel formulas** (Excel does the arithmetic),
+  it embeds **no answer data** — every number it writes is a live formula, and all
+  numeric parameters (`--investment`, `--fx`, `--multiplier`, …) come from the task
+  statement. It is NOT "computing the answer in Python". Hand-building with openpyxl
+  writes the same formulas but risks getting the layout wrong.
+- The scaffolder loads a provided template in place (preserving its `NA` sheet, year
+  rows, and assumption labels) and only adds the sheets that are missing.
+- Do this even if you have not yet collected the IMF/Geostat data — scaffold first,
+  then paste the actuals into the blank cells and run `python recalc.py <target.xlsx>`.
+
+After scaffolding, see `references/shock_analysis_model.md` for the full layout and the
+data fill-in steps. Only if the task is NOT this economic-shock type, continue below.
