+## Macro-accounting shock / impact-analysis workbooks
+
+If the task asks you to estimate an **investment or spending shock** to an economy
+using the **macro accounting (demand-side) framework** — building an Excel model
+with IMF **WEO** GDP projections, a statistics-office **supply-use table**, and a
+**National-Accounts (NA)** impact table with multiple scenarios (required sheets
+such as `WEO_Data`, `SUT Calc`, `SUPPLY (…)`, `USE (…)`, `NA`) — **do not hand-build
+the sheet skeleton.** Run the bundled scaffolder, which lays out every sheet, column
+order, and projection/cross-sheet formula correctly, then paste in the data you
+collected:
+
+```bash
+python scripts/build_shock_model.py --out <target.xlsx> \
+    --investment <amount> --fx <rate> --multiplier <m> \
+    --scenario2-multiplier <m2> --scenario3-import-share <s3>
+```
+
+All parameters come from the task statement (the script embeds **no answer data**,
+only structure). It preserves any provided template sheet and fills it in place.
+See `references/shock_analysis_model.md` for the full layout and the fill-in steps.
+Then paste the collected actuals into the blank cells and run `recalc.py`.
+
 ## CRITICAL: Use Formulas, Not Hardcoded Values
 
 **Always use Excel formulas instead of calculating values in Python and hardcoding them.** This ensures the spreadsheet remains dynamic and updateable.
